Always Reference the README

Before generating or updating code, ensure the feature is listed in the README’s “MVP Features” or “Roadmap.”

New features must first be approved and added to the README spec.

Follow the File & Folder Structure Exactly

All components go under src/components/ named as specified (Onboarding.jsx, Home.jsx, etc.).

Configuration files stay at project root (tailwind.config.js, postcss.config.js, .gitignore).

Assets live in src/assets/ and referenced by relative paths.

Styling & Theming

Use Tailwind CSS utility classes exclusively—no inline CSS or other frameworks.

Use only the defined brand colors:

Petal Pink: #FADADD

Blossom: #DB2777

Mint Green: #D4EADF

Warm Neutral: #F5F1EA

Soft Gray: #9CA3AF

Adhere to the border‐radius, shadow, and spacing conventions in the Brand Guidelines.

Brand Guidelines:
# CoreBloom Brand Guidelines

A comprehensive guide to the visual and verbal identity of CoreBloom, the postpartum strength training app.

---

## 1. Logo & Symbol

* **Primary Logo**: A stylized blooming flower with three petals, symbolizing the three core workouts (A, B, C). The petals overlap slightly, creating a soft, organic shape.
* **Logo Usage**:

  * **Full Logo**: Flower symbol + wordmark “CoreBloom” in chosen font.
  * **Symbol Only**: Use the flower icon alone for app icon, social avatars, and small-format needs.
* **Clear Space**: Maintain at least the height of one petal around the logo on all sides.
* **Minimum Size**: 32px height for digital, 0.5in for print.

---

## 2. Color Palette

| Name             | Hex     | Usage                         |
| ---------------- | ------- | ----------------------------- |
| **Petal Pink**   | #FADADD | Primary buttons, highlights   |
| **Blossom**      | #DB2777 | Active states, icons, accents |
| **Mint Green**   | #D4EADF | Secondary backgrounds, cards  |
| **Warm Neutral** | #F5F1EA | Page backgrounds, modals      |
| **Soft Gray**    | #9CA3AF | Text, secondary UI elements   |

Use high-contrast combinations for accessibility: Blossom (#DB2777) on Warm Neutral (#F5F1EA) for headings.

---

## 3. Typography

* **Primary Font**: **Poppins** (Google Font)

  * **Weights**: 400 (Regular), 600 (SemiBold), 700 (Bold)
  * **Usage**:

    * **Headlines**: Poppins 600, 24–32px
    * **Body Text**: Poppins 400, 14–18px
    * **Buttons & Labels**: Poppins 600, 16px
* **Fallbacks**: `-apple-system`, `BlinkMacSystemFont`, `Segoe UI`, `Roboto`, `Helvetica Neue`, `Arial`, `sans-serif`

---

## 4. Iconography

* **Style**: Simple line icons with rounded ends, consistent stroke weight.
* **Library**: **lucide-react** for digital use.
* **Color**: Use **Blossom** for active icons, **Soft Gray** for inactive.

---

## 5. Imagery & Illustrations

* **Photography**:

  * Natural, warm lighting.
  * Real postpartum bodies in motion.
  * Candid, supportive, empowering moments.
* **Illustrations**:

  * Flat, minimalist shapes.
  * Brand palette (use exclusively the defined colors).
  * Emphasize gentle curves and motion lines.

---

## 6. Tone of Voice

* **Personality**: Warm, encouraging, empowering.
* **Style**:

  * Address the user directly (“you”).
  * Keep messages short and positive.
  * Sprinkle friendly emojis sparingly (🌸, 💪).
* **Example**:

  > “You’re doing great—one step at a time!”

---

## 7. UI Components

* **Buttons**: Rounded corners (2xl), solid Petal Pink background, white text.
* **Cards**: Mint Green background or white, rounded-2xl, soft shadow.
* **Inputs**: White backgrounds, 1px Soft Gray border, rounded-xl, focus ring Blossom.
* **Tabs**: Bottom nav with icons; active tab in Blossom, inactive in Soft Gray.

---

## 8. Usage Examples

* **App Icon**: Symbol only on a Mint Green square, rounded corners.
* **Splash Screen**: Full logo centered on Warm Neutral.
* **Marketing Graphic**:

  * Left: Illustration of a mom performing an exercise.
  * Right: Headline in Poppins 700, Blossom color.

---

*These guidelines ensure consistency and brand recognition across all CoreBloom touchpoints.*


Component API & Behavior

All state lives in React useState or useEffect hooks.

Persist user data only to localStorage keys profile and workoutsDone; never fetch external APIs.

Navigation uses React Router v6 <Routes>, <Route>, and <Outlet> exactly.

Bottom tab bar must use <NavLink> or <Link> with active styling matching Brand Guidelines.

Naming & Exports

Components must default‑export a single function matching filename (e.g. export default function WorkoutA()).

Files and component names are PascalCase; CSS classes remain lowercase hyphen‑separated as Tailwind utilities.

Commit & Code Style

Generate code with 2‑space indentation, single quotes for strings, semicolons on every statement.

Include a brief JSDoc comment on each component file summarizing its purpose.

When creating or modifying code, include a one‑sentence “chore:” or “feat:” summary at the top of the change.

Error Handling & Guards

If reading localStorage returns null or malformed JSON, fallback gracefully (e.g. || '{}' or || '[]').

In nested workout routes, if the user refreshes mid‑workout, ensure the component still renders without crashing.

Testing & Validation

After each new component or route, verify manually (or via a simple smoke test) that:

Onboarding redirects correctly when profile exists or not.

Home cycles workouts A→B→C based on count of workoutsDone.

“Mark Complete” truly updates localStorage.

For any UI changes, test at both desktop and a 375px‑wide mobile viewport.

No Scope Creep

Do not add features beyond the MVP unless explicitly requested.

Any roadmap items must be scoped in a separate issue or added to the README before implementation.

Documentation First

For any new feature, update the README spec with the exact behaviour and UI mockup first.

Only after the README is updated should the AI generate code for that feature.

